-- title:
--   Theorem 6.1 — a limit of δ-locally optimal progressive hedging is a stationary point of (P) and a local solution of (P̃)
-- statement:
--   Consider the scenario model under its standing assumptions, with no convexity. Fix $r>0$ and $\delta>0$, and let $(X^\nu)$, $(W^\nu)$ be generated by the progressive hedging algorithm in such a way that in each iteration $X^{\nu+1}(s)$ is $\delta$-locally optimal for the scenario subproblem
--   $$(P^\nu_s)\qquad \text{minimize } f_s(x)+x\cdot W^\nu(s)+\tfrac12 r|x-\hat X^\nu(s)|^2 \text{ over } x\in C_s ,$$
--   with $\hat X^\nu=JX^\nu$ and $W^{\nu+1}=W^\nu+rKX^{\nu+1}$. Suppose $X^\nu\to X^*$ and $W^\nu\to W^*$. Then:
--
--   1. $X^*$ and $W^*$ satisfy the optimality conditions of Theorem 4.1: $X^*\in\mathcal N$, $X^*\in\mathcal C$, $W^*\in\mathcal M$ and
--   $$-W^*(s)\in\partial f_s(X^*(s))+N_{C_s}(X^*(s))\qquad\text{for all } s\in S;$$
--   in this sense $X^*$ is a stationary point of (P);
--   2. $X^*$ is a locally optimal solution of the problem $(\tilde P)$ obtained from (P) by replacing each $f_s$ by
--   $$\tilde f_s(x)=f_s(x)+\tfrac12 r|x-X^*(s)|^2, \tag{6.1}$$
--   that is, $\sum_s p_s\tilde f_s(X^*(s))\le\sum_s p_s\tilde f_s(X(s))$ for all $X\in\mathcal C\cap\mathcal N$ near $X^*$;
--   3. $W^*$ is a corresponding multiplier vector for $(\tilde P)$: $-W^*(s)\in\partial\tilde f_s(X^*(s))+N_{C_s}(X^*(s))$ for all $s\in S$.
--
--   Here $\partial$ is Clarke's generalized gradient and $N_C$ Clarke's normal cone. Without convexity one cannot expect the algorithm to solve (P) globally; the theorem says that whenever it converges, its limit is as good a "solution" as can be hoped for.
--
--   **Formalization Note** No convexity and no constraint qualification are assumed: the standing assumptions of the model (nonempty closed $C_s$, locally Lipschitz $f_s$, bounded level sets) are the only hypotheses besides the run and its convergence. The $\delta$-neighbourhood of $(P^\nu_s)$ is the closed Euclidean ball. Convergence of policies and "near $X^*$" refer to the (norm-independent) topology of the finite-dimensional space $\mathcal E$. The algorithm starts from $X^0\in\mathcal C$ and $W^0\in\mathcal M$ as on p. 9.
-- source:
--   Rockafellar and Wets, Scenarios and policy aggregation in optimization under uncertainty, IIASA Working Paper WP-87-119 (1987), p. 30, Theorem 6.1

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_ProgHedging_Convex_Problem
import Definitions.Def_ProgHedging_Nonconvex_Algorithm
import Definitions.Def_ClarkeGradients_Shared_generalizedGradient
import Definitions.Def_ClarkeGradients_FlowInvariance_normalCone

open scoped Pointwise
open Filter Topology

namespace ProgHedging.Nonconvex

/-- Theorem 6.1, p. 30: if progressive hedging with `δ`-locally optimal subproblem solutions
generates `X^ν → X*` and `W^ν → W*`, then `X*`, `W*` satisfy the optimality conditions of
Theorem 4.1; moreover `X*` is locally optimal for, and `W*` a multiplier vector for, the problem
`(P̃)` obtained by replacing each `f_s` with `f̃_s(x) = f_s(x) + ½r|x − X*(s)|²` (6.1). -/
theorem theorem_6_1 {S : Type*} [Fintype S] {n T : ℕ} (pr : Problem S n T) {r δ : ℝ}
    (hr : 0 < r) (hδ : 0 < δ) (X W : ℕ → ProgHedging.Convex.Policy S n) (hseq : pr.IsLocalPHSeq r δ X W)
    (Xs Ws : ProgHedging.Convex.Policy S n) (hX : Tendsto X atTop (𝓝 Xs)) (hW : Tendsto W atTop (𝓝 Ws)) :
    pr.OptCond41 Xs Ws ∧
    (∃ ε > 0, ∀ Y ∈ pr.adm ∩ pr.N, dist Y Xs < ε →
      ∑ s, pr.p s * pr.fTilde r Xs s (Xs s) ≤ ∑ s, pr.p s * pr.fTilde r Xs s (Y s)) ∧
    ∀ s, -Ws s ∈ ClarkeGradients.Shared.generalizedGradient (pr.fTilde r Xs s) (Xs s) +
      ClarkeGradients.FlowInvariance.normalCone (pr.C s) (Xs s) := by sorry

end ProgHedging.Nonconvex
